/* ========== LEVEL 2 · THEORY (A-level) ========== */
U({id:'t2-1',track:'theory',level:2,title:'Inside the nucleus',icon:'⚛︎',color:'#7F77DD',desc:'Why some nuclei are stable and others aren\'t, the different ways they decay, and decay chains.',lessons:[
LH('t2-1-1','What makes a nucleus stable?',9,'Why carbon-12 lasts for ever but carbon-14 doesn\'t: the balance inside the nucleus.',[
RM(`<ul><li>A nucleus contains <b>protons</b> (positive) and <b>neutrons</b> (no charge).</li><li>The <b>strong nuclear force</b> pulls them together, but only over tiny distances. Protons also <b>push each other apart</b> electrically.</li><li><b>Isotopes</b> of an element have the same protons but different numbers of neutrons.</li></ul>`),
T(`<h3>A balancing act</h3><p>Every nucleus is a balance between two forces:</p><ul><li>the <b>strong force</b>, pulling all protons and neutrons together (short range: only between near neighbours)</li><li>the <b>electric push</b> between protons (long range: every proton pushes on every other proton)</li></ul><p>Neutrons add pull without adding push. So the right number of neutrons keeps a nucleus together. Too few or too many, and it's <b>unstable</b>.</p>`),
T(`<h3>The line of stability</h3><p>If you plot every known nucleus by its number of neutrons (N) against protons (Z), the stable ones form a narrow band called the <b>line of stability</b>.</p><ul><li>Light nuclei are stable with about <b>equal</b> numbers: carbon-12 has 6 protons and 6 neutrons.</li><li>Heavy nuclei need <b>more neutrons than protons</b> to dilute the push: lead-208 has 82 protons and 126 neutrons.</li><li>Above lead (82 protons), no nucleus is truly stable. Uranium is radioactive, just very slowly.</li></ul>`),
W('fnPlot',{x:[0,150],y:[0,100],xl:'neutrons (N)',yl:'protons (Z)',sl:[['z','Element (protons, Z)',6,92,2,26,function(v){return v}]],curves:function(p){return [[function(n){return n},'--muted',1.5,[6,5]],[function(n){return stableZ(n)},'--accent',3]]},marks:function(p,P,col){var n=stableN(p.z),c=P.c;c.fillStyle=col('--coral');c.beginPath();c.arc(P.X(n),P.Y(p.z),6,0,7);c.fill()},read:function(p){var n=Math.round(stableN(p.z));return 'With <b>'+p.z+' protons</b>, the most stable nuclei have about <b>'+n+' neutrons</b>: '+(n-p.z)+' more than protons. The dashed line is N = Z.'}},`Move along the line of stability. Watch how heavy nuclei need more neutrons than protons.`,`The heavier the nucleus, the further the stable line bends away from N = Z.`),
Q(`Why do heavy nuclei need more neutrons than protons?`,[`Neutrons add strong-force pull without adding electric push`,'Neutrons are heavier, so they hold the nucleus down','Protons are unstable, so extra neutrons replace them','Electrons need room, and neutrons make the atom bigger'],0,`Every extra proton pushes on all the others. Extra neutrons help hold everything together.`),
T(`<h3>Too many or too few neutrons</h3><p>A nucleus off the line of stability will decay to get closer to it:</p><table><tr><th>Problem</th><th>Fix</th><th>Example</th></tr><tr><td>Too many neutrons</td><td>A neutron turns into a proton: <b>beta-minus</b> decay</td><td>Fission products, like iodine-131</td></tr><tr><td>Too many protons</td><td>A proton turns into a neutron: <b>beta-plus</b> decay or electron capture</td><td>Fluorine-18 (used in PET scans)</td></tr><tr><td>Far too heavy</td><td>Throw out an alpha particle</td><td>Uranium-238, plutonium-239</td></tr></table>`),
Q(`Fission products have too many neutrons for their size. How do they usually decay?`,[`Beta-minus: a neutron turns into a proton`,'Alpha: the nucleus throws out two protons and two neutrons','Beta-plus: a proton turns into a neutron','They don\'t decay: extra neutrons make them stable'],0,`That's why used fuel gives off so much beta and gamma radiation.`),
QE(`Which radiation is stopped by a few millimetres of aluminium?`,[`Beta`,`Alpha`,`Gamma`,`Neutrons`],0,`From Level 1: alpha is stopped by paper, beta by aluminium.`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li>Stability is a balance: strong-force pull vs electric push between protons.</li><li>Light stable nuclei have N ≈ Z; heavy ones need <b>more neutrons</b>.</li><li>Too many neutrons → <b>beta-minus</b>. Too many protons → <b>beta-plus</b>. Far too heavy → <b>alpha</b>.</li></ul>`)]),
LH('t2-1-2','The ways a nucleus can decay',9,'Alpha, beta-minus, beta-plus, electron capture and gamma: what changes in each.',[
RM(`<p>A nucleus is written with its mass number (protons + neutrons) and the element. Uranium-238 has 92 protons and 146 neutrons.</p><p>Unstable nuclei decay to move closer to the line of stability.</p>`),
T(`<h3>Keeping count</h3><p>In every decay, two things are always conserved:</p><ul><li>the <b>mass number</b> (total protons + neutrons) on each side</li><li>the <b>charge</b> (number of protons) on each side, counting any particles thrown out</li></ul><p>Use these to work out what the new nucleus is.</p>`),
T(`<h3>The decay types</h3><table><tr><th>Decay</th><th>Mass number</th><th>Protons</th></tr><tr><td><b>Alpha (α)</b>: throws out 2 protons + 2 neutrons</td><td>−4</td><td>−2</td></tr><tr><td><b>Beta-minus (β⁻)</b>: a neutron becomes a proton, an electron flies out</td><td>same</td><td>+1</td></tr><tr><td><b>Beta-plus (β⁺)</b>: a proton becomes a neutron, a positron flies out</td><td>same</td><td>−1</td></tr><tr><td><b>Electron capture</b>: the nucleus grabs an inner electron, a proton becomes a neutron</td><td>same</td><td>−1</td></tr><tr><td><b>Gamma (γ)</b>: leftover energy leaves as a photon</td><td>same</td><td>same</td></tr></table>`),
W('matchPairs',{pairs:[['Alpha','Mass number −4, protons −2'],['Beta-minus','Neutron → proton, protons +1'],['Beta-plus','Proton → neutron, protons −1'],['Gamma','No change in protons or mass number'],['Electron capture','Grabs an electron: protons −1']]},`Match each decay to what it changes.`,`Only alpha changes the mass number. Beta and electron capture swap a neutron and a proton. Gamma just releases energy.`),
W('stepCalc',{steps:['Uranium-238 (92 protons) decays by alpha. What does it become?','Mass number: 238 − 4 = 234','Protons: 92 − 2 = 90','The element with 90 protons is thorium.','So U-238 → <b>Th-234</b> + an alpha particle. Mass: 238 = 234 + 4 ✓. Protons: 92 = 90 + 2 ✓.']},`Work out an alpha decay step by step.`),
Q(`Iodine-131 (53 protons) decays by beta-minus. What does it become?`,[`Xenon-131 (54 protons)`,`Tellurium-131 (52 protons)`,`Iodine-130`,`Antimony-127`],0,`Beta-minus: mass number stays 131, protons go up by 1 to 54, which is xenon.`),
QE(`What is a half-life?`,[`The time for half of the unstable nuclei to decay`,`Half the time a reactor can run before refuelling`,`The time for all of the unstable nuclei to decay`,`Half the mass of the unstable nuclei in a sample`],0,`From Level 1.`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li>Mass number and charge are <b>conserved</b> in every decay.</li><li>Alpha: −4 mass, −2 protons. Beta-minus: +1 proton. Beta-plus and electron capture: −1 proton. Gamma: no change.</li><li>Gamma usually follows another decay, carrying away leftover energy.</li></ul>`)]),
LH('t2-1-3','Decay chains',10,'One decay leads to another: the chain that produces xenon-135, and why it matters for operators.',[
RM(`<ul><li>Fission products have too many neutrons, so they decay by <b>beta-minus</b> (a neutron becomes a proton, mass number unchanged).</li><li><b>Xenon-135</b> is a powerful neutron absorber that builds up after a trip (Level 1, Controlling the reactor).</li></ul>`),
T(`<h3>One decay after another</h3><p>A fission product often needs several beta-minus decays to reach stability. Each step makes a new element with the same mass number. This is a <b>decay chain</b>.</p><p>The most important chain for operators has mass number 135:</p>`),
O(`Put the mass-135 chain in order (each step is a beta-minus decay).`,[`Tellurium-135 (half-life 19 s)`,`Iodine-135 (half-life 6.6 hours)`,`Xenon-135 (half-life 9.1 hours)`,`Caesium-135 (half-life 2 million years)`],`Protons go up by one at each step: 52 → 53 → 54 → 55.`),
T(`<h3>Why the chain explains the xenon peak</h3><p>Most xenon-135 is made <b>indirectly</b>: fission makes tellurium-135, which quickly becomes iodine-135, which slowly (6.6 h half-life) becomes xenon-135.</p><p>At power, xenon is also <b>burnt away</b> by absorbing neutrons. After a trip:</p><ul><li>the neutrons stop, so xenon is no longer burnt away</li><li>but the iodine already in the fuel keeps decaying into xenon for hours</li></ul><p>So xenon <b>rises</b> for roughly 8 to 10 hours, then falls as it decays.</p>`),
W('fnPlot',{x:[0,48],y:[0,2.4],xl:'hours after trip',yl:'xenon (× full-power level)',sl:[['t','Time after trip (h)',0,48,1,0,function(v){return v+' h'}]],curves:function(p){return [[xeTrip,'--accent',3]]},marks:function(p,P,col){var c=P.c;c.fillStyle=col('--coral');c.beginPath();c.arc(P.X(p.t),P.Y(xeTrip(p.t)),6,0,7);c.fill()},read:function(p){var y=xeTrip(p.t);return p.t+' h after the trip, xenon is <b>'+y.toFixed(2)+' ×</b> its full-power level. '+(p.t<7?'Still rising: iodine is feeding it.':p.t<=10?'Around the peak.':'Falling: the iodine is used up and xenon decays.')},goal:function(p){return p.t>=7&&p.t<=10},goalMsg:'Find the peak: move the slider to where xenon is highest.'},`Move through the hours after a trip from full power. Find the xenon peak.`,`Xenon peaks at nearly twice its full-power level about 8 to 9 hours after the trip, then decays away over the next day or two.`),
Q(`Why does xenon keep rising for hours after a trip?`,[`Iodine-135 already in the fuel keeps decaying into xenon, but it's no longer burnt away by neutrons`,'Fission keeps going for hours after the trip at low power, so xenon keeps being made directly from it','The control rods make xenon as they absorb neutrons, and they\'re now fully in','Operators add xenon to the coolant after a trip to keep the reactor shut down'],0,`Production carries on (from iodine) while removal by neutrons stops.`),
QE(`Iodine-135 decays by beta-minus. Its mass number…`,[`stays 135`,`goes down by 4`,`goes up by 1`,`goes down by 1`],0,`Beta decay doesn't change the mass number.`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li>A <b>decay chain</b> is a series of decays, each making a new element.</li><li>Mass-135 chain: Te-135 → <b>I-135</b> (6.6 h) → <b>Xe-135</b> (9.1 h) → Cs-135.</li><li>After a trip, iodine keeps feeding xenon while neutrons stop burning it: xenon <b>peaks after about 8 to 10 hours</b>.</li></ul>`)])
]});
F('t2-1-1',[T(`<h3>Quick recap</h3><p>Stability = strong-force pull vs electric push. Heavy nuclei need extra neutrons.</p>`),Q(`Which force pushes protons apart?`,[`The electric force`,`The strong force`,`Gravity`,`Friction`],0,`Like charges repel.`),Q(`A nucleus has too many protons. It might decay by…`,[`beta-plus or electron capture`,`beta-minus`,`fission`,`nothing`],0,`A proton turns into a neutron.`),Q(`Above which element are no nuclei truly stable?`,[`Lead (82 protons)`,`Iron`,`Carbon`,`Hydrogen`],0,`Everything heavier is radioactive.`)]);
F('t2-1-2',[T(`<h3>Quick recap</h3><p>Alpha −4/−2; β⁻ +1 proton; β⁺/EC −1 proton; γ no change.</p>`),Q(`Radium-226 (88 protons) alpha decays. It becomes…`,[`Radon-222 (86 protons)`,`Radium-222`,`Actinium-226`,`Thorium-230`],0,`226 − 4 = 222; 88 − 2 = 86.`),Q(`Which decay changes the mass number?`,[`Alpha`,`Beta-minus`,`Gamma`,`Electron capture`],0,`Only alpha.`),Q(`What is always conserved in a decay?`,[`Mass number and charge`,`The element`,`The number of neutrons`,`Nothing`],0,`Count both sides.`)]);
F('t2-1-3',[T(`<h3>Quick recap</h3><p>Te-135 → I-135 → Xe-135 → Cs-135. Xenon peaks about 8–10 h after a trip.</p>`),Q(`Which has a 6.6 hour half-life?`,[`Iodine-135`,`Xenon-135`,`Tellurium-135`,`Caesium-135`],0,`Iodine feeds xenon.`),Q(`At full power, xenon is removed by…`,[`decaying and absorbing neutrons (burnout)`,`the control rods`,`boron`,`the condenser`],0,`Two removal routes.`),Q(`After the peak, xenon falls because…`,[`the iodine is used up and xenon decays`,`the rods are withdrawn`,`boron is added`,`it boils away`],0,`Production drops, decay continues.`)]);
TT('t2-1',[x('t2-1-1',Q(`Light stable nuclei have…`,[`roughly equal protons and neutrons`,`no neutrons`,`twice as many protons`,`no protons`],0,`N ≈ Z.`)),x('t2-1-2',Q(`Carbon-14 (6 protons) beta-minus decays to…`,[`Nitrogen-14`,`Boron-14`,`Carbon-13`,`Beryllium-10`],0,`Protons 6 → 7.`)),x('t2-1-2',Q(`Gamma decay changes…`,[`only the energy of the nucleus`,`the element`,`the mass number`,`the number of neutrons`],0,`It releases energy.`)),x('t2-1-3',Q(`Most xenon-135 comes from…`,[`the decay of iodine-135`,`direct fission only`,`the coolant`,`control rods`],0,`Indirectly.`)),x('t2-1-3',Q(`Roughly when does xenon peak after a trip from full power?`,[`About 8 to 10 hours`,`Instantly`,`After a week`,`After 1 minute`],0,`Then it decays over a day or two.`)),x('t2-1-1',Q(`Fission products are on which side of the line of stability?`,[`Too many neutrons`,`Too many protons`,`Exactly on it`,`Too few neutrons`],0,`So they beta-minus decay.`))]);

U({id:'t2-2',track:'theory',level:2,title:'How neutrons interact',icon:'σ',color:'#378ADD',desc:'Scattering, capture and fission; cross-sections as target size; why slow neutrons are captured more.',lessons:[
LH('t2-2-1','Scatter or be absorbed',9,'Every time a neutron meets a nucleus, one of a few things happens. Learn them all.',[
RM(`<ul><li>Fission neutrons are <b>fast</b>; the moderator (water) slows them down by collisions.</li><li>Control rods and boron are <b>absorbers</b>: they soak up neutrons.</li></ul>`),
T(`<h3>Two families of interaction</h3><p>When a neutron meets a nucleus, it is either <b>scattered</b> (it bounces off and carries on) or <b>absorbed</b> (it disappears into the nucleus).</p><table><tr><th>Interaction</th><th>What happens</th></tr><tr><td><b>Elastic scattering</b></td><td>Bounces off like snooker balls. Total movement energy is kept. This is how the moderator slows neutrons.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Inelastic scattering</b></td><td>Bounces off, but leaves the nucleus excited; the nucleus then gives off a gamma ray. Needs a fast neutron.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Radiative capture</b></td><td>The neutron is absorbed and a gamma ray comes out. The nucleus becomes a heavier isotope.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Fission</b></td><td>The neutron is absorbed and the nucleus splits.</td></tr></table>`),
W('sortIt',{bins:['Scattering','Absorption'],items:[['A neutron bounces off a hydrogen nucleus in water',0],['A fast neutron bounces off iron, leaving it excited',0],['Boron-10 swallows a neutron and splits into lithium and helium',1],['Uranium-238 captures a neutron and becomes uranium-239',1],['Uranium-235 absorbs a neutron and splits',1],['A neutron bounces off an oxygen nucleus',0]]},`Scattering or absorption?`,`Scattering: the neutron carries on. Absorption: the neutron is gone.`),
Q(`Which interaction slows neutrons down in a PWR?`,[`Elastic scattering off hydrogen`,'Radiative capture by uranium-238','Fission of uranium-235 nuclei','Absorption by the boron in the water'],0,`Each bounce off hydrogen takes a large share of the neutron's energy.`),
T(`<h3>Absorption isn't always bad</h3><p>Capture "wastes" a neutron as far as the chain reaction goes, but it's also how we <b>control</b> the reactor (boron, control rods) and how <b>plutonium</b> is made (uranium-238 capture).</p><p>It also makes materials <b>radioactive</b>: steel and corrosion products in the primary circuit become activated by capturing neutrons.</p>`),
Q(`Why does the primary circuit become slightly radioactive?`,[`Materials in and near the core capture neutrons and become radioactive isotopes`,'The water dissolves some uranium from the fuel pellets as it flows past','Radiation from the core sticks to the pipe walls and builds up over time','It doesn\'t: the primary water stays completely non-radioactive'],0,`Neutron capture creates activation products like cobalt-60 from steel.`),
QE(`Which material slows neutrons best: lead, water or air?`,[`Water`,`Lead`,`Air`,`They're all the same`],0,`From Level 1: hydrogen is about the same mass as a neutron.`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li><b>Scattering</b>: elastic (moderation) or inelastic (fast neutrons, gamma emitted).</li><li><b>Absorption</b>: radiative capture or fission.</li><li>Capture is used for control, makes plutonium, and activates materials.</li></ul>`)]),
LH('t2-2-2','Cross-sections: how big is the target?',10,'A way of measuring how likely a neutron is to hit a nucleus, using a very odd unit: the barn.',[
RM(`<p>A neutron can scatter, be captured, or cause fission. Some materials (boron, xenon) absorb neutrons far more readily than others (zirconium, water).</p>`),
T(`<h3>Imagine every nucleus as a target</h3><p>How likely is a neutron to interact with a nucleus? We pretend each nucleus is a little disc, facing the neutron. The bigger the disc, the more likely a hit.</p><p>The area of that imaginary disc is the <b>microscopic cross-section</b>, written <b>σ</b> (sigma).</p><div class="callout">It's not the real size of the nucleus. It's a way of measuring <b>probability</b> as an area.</div>`),
T(`<h3>The barn</h3><p>Cross-sections are tiny, so they're measured in <b>barns</b>:</p><div class="eq">1 barn = 10<sup>−24</sup> cm²</div><p>(American physicists in the 1940s joked that uranium was "as big as a barn" for neutrons. The name stuck.)</p><table><tr><th>Nucleus</th><th>Absorption cross-section for slow neutrons</th></tr><tr><td>Zirconium</td><td>0.18 barns (almost invisible: good for cladding)</td></tr><tr><td>Hydrogen</td><td>0.33 barns</td></tr><tr><td>Uranium-235 (fission)</td><td>about 585 barns</td></tr><tr><td>Boron-10</td><td>about 3,840 barns (a great absorber)</td></tr><tr><td>Xenon-135</td><td>about 2,600,000 barns!</td></tr></table>`),
Q(`Why is zirconium used for fuel cladding?`,[`Its absorption cross-section is tiny, so it hardly wastes neutrons`,'Its absorption cross-section is huge, so it soaks up spare neutrons','It\'s radioactive, so it adds extra heat to the fuel','It\'s a good moderator, so it slows neutrons for fission'],0,`0.18 barns: neutrons pass straight through.`),
W('flipCards',{cards:[['σ','The microscopic cross-section: the "target area" of one nucleus'],['1 barn','10<sup>−24</sup> cm²'],['Boron-10','About 3,840 barns: a strong absorber for control'],['Xenon-135','About 2.6 million barns: the strongest known absorber']]},`Tap to flip each card.`),
T(`<h3>Different cross-sections for different things</h3><p>Each nucleus has a separate cross-section for each interaction: σ<sub>s</sub> for scattering, σ<sub>c</sub> for capture, σ<sub>f</sub> for fission. Add the absorption ones to get σ<sub>a</sub>.</p><p>And they all depend strongly on the neutron's <b>speed</b>, which is the next lesson.</p>`),
QE(`Which fission product is a strong neutron absorber that builds up after a trip?`,[`Xenon-135`,`Caesium-137`,`Iodine-131`,`Strontium-90`],0,`And now you know why: its cross-section is millions of barns.`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li>The <b>cross-section σ</b> measures how likely an interaction is, as an imaginary target area.</li><li>Measured in <b>barns</b>: 1 b = 10⁻²⁴ cm².</li><li>Separate σ for scattering, capture and fission. Zirconium is tiny; boron and xenon are huge.</li></ul>`)]),
LH('t2-2-3','Slow neutrons are easier to catch',10,'How cross-sections change with neutron speed, and what resonances are.',[
RM(`<ul><li>The cross-section σ is a nucleus's "target area" for neutrons, measured in barns.</li><li>Fission neutrons are fast (about 2 MeV); thermal neutrons in a PWR are slow (about 0.025 eV).</li></ul>`),
T(`<h3>The 1/v rule</h3><p>For many nuclei, the slower the neutron, the bigger the absorption cross-section. A slow neutron spends longer near the nucleus, so it's more likely to be caught.</p><div class="eq">σ<sub>a</sub> ∝ 1 / v</div>${KY([['σ<sub>a</sub>','absorption cross-section','barns'],['v','neutron speed','m/s']])}<p>That's the reason a PWR needs a moderator: uranium-235's fission cross-section is hundreds of times bigger for thermal neutrons than for fast ones.</p>`),
T(`<h3>Resonances: sharp peaks</h3><p>At certain exact energies, a neutron fits perfectly into an energy level of the nucleus, and the cross-section shoots up. These narrow peaks are called <b>resonances</b>.</p><p>Uranium-238 has huge capture resonances (the first at 6.67 eV). Neutrons slowing down through those energies risk being captured by U-238 instead of reaching thermal energy.</p>`),
W('fnPlot',{x:[0.01,1000],xlog:true,ylog:true,y:[1,100000],xl:'neutron energy (eV), log scale',yl:'U-238 capture σ (barns)',sl:[['e','Neutron energy (power of ten)',-2,3,0.25,-1.5,function(v){return Math.pow(10,v)<1?Math.pow(10,v).toFixed(3)+' eV':Math.pow(10,v).toFixed(0)+' eV'}]],curves:function(p){return [[function(E){var s=2.7*Math.sqrt(0.0253/E);[[6.67,7000],[20.9,9000],[36.7,8000],[66,6000],[80.7,3000],[102.5,5000],[116.9,4000],[189.6,2500],[208.5,3000]].forEach(function(r){var d=(E-r[0])/(0.004*r[0]);s+=r[1]/(1+d*d)});return s},'--coral',2.2]]},marks:function(p,P,col){var E=Math.pow(10,p.e),c=P.c;c.strokeStyle=col('--accent');c.lineWidth=2;c.beginPath();c.moveTo(P.X(E),P.Y(1));c.lineTo(P.X(E),P.Y(100000));c.stroke()},read:function(p){var E=Math.pow(10,p.e);return E<1?'<b>Thermal / slow</b> region: the smooth 1/v curve.':E<4?'Just below the first big resonance.':'<b>Resonance region</b>: sharp peaks where U-238 captures neutrons very strongly.'},goal:function(p){var E=Math.pow(10,p.e);return E>=4&&E<=12},goalMsg:'Move the marker to the first big resonance, near 6.7 eV.'},`Explore uranium-238's capture cross-section. Move the marker onto the first big resonance (near 6.7 eV).`,`Smooth 1/v at low energy, then sharp resonance peaks. Neutrons must get past these peaks to become thermal.`),
Q(`What is a resonance?`,[`A narrow energy where a cross-section shoots up`,'A humming sound made by the reactor','A type of fission caused by fast neutrons','The energy of a neutron after it slows down'],0,`At a resonance, the neutron's energy fits an energy level of the nucleus.`),
Q(`Why does the 1/v rule mean a PWR needs a moderator?`,[`Slow neutrons are far more likely to cause fission in U-235`,'Fast neutrons are absorbed by water before they reach the fuel','Slow neutrons escape the core, so they must be sped back up','It doesn\'t: fast neutrons cause fission just as easily'],0,`Slowing neutrons down makes the fission cross-section hundreds of times bigger.`),
QE(`What is 1 barn?`,[`10<sup>−24</sup> cm²`,`1 cm²`,`10<sup>−24</sup> kg`,`A unit of energy`],0,`From the last lesson.`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li>Many cross-sections follow <b>1/v</b>: slower neutrons are caught more often.</li><li><b>Resonances</b> are sharp peaks at particular energies. U-238's capture resonances start at 6.67 eV.</li><li>Neutrons slowing down must "escape" the resonances to reach thermal energy.</li></ul>`)])
]});
F('t2-2-1',[T(`<h3>Quick recap</h3><p>Scattering (elastic, inelastic) or absorption (capture, fission).</p>`),Q(`Inelastic scattering needs…`,[`a fast neutron`,`a thermal neutron`,`boron`,`a gamma ray`],0,`Enough energy to excite the nucleus.`),Q(`Radiative capture gives off…`,[`a gamma ray`,`an alpha particle`,`two fragments`,`nothing`],0,`The new nucleus releases energy as gamma.`),Q(`Which is an absorption reaction?`,[`Fission`,`Elastic scattering`,`Inelastic scattering`,`Moderation`],0,`The neutron is absorbed.`)]);
F('t2-2-2',[T(`<h3>Quick recap</h3><p>σ = target area for one nucleus, in barns (10⁻²⁴ cm²).</p>`),Q(`A bigger cross-section means…`,[`a more likely interaction`,`a bigger real nucleus`,`a less likely interaction`,`a faster neutron`],0,`It measures probability.`),Q(`Which has the biggest absorption cross-section?`,[`Xenon-135`,`Boron-10`,`Hydrogen`,`Zirconium`],0,`About 2.6 million barns.`),Q(`σ<sub>f</sub> means…`,[`the fission cross-section`,`the scattering cross-section`,`fast flux`,`fuel temperature`],0,`f for fission.`)]);
F('t2-2-3',[T(`<h3>Quick recap</h3><p>1/v: slower = more likely to be absorbed. Resonances: sharp peaks.</p>`),Q(`Halving a neutron's speed (1/v region) makes its absorption cross-section…`,[`double`,`halve`,`quarter`,`stay the same`],0,`σ ∝ 1/v.`),Q(`Where is U-238's first big capture resonance?`,[`About 6.67 eV`,`About 2 MeV`,`About 0.025 eV`,`About 1 keV`],0,`In the slowing-down range.`),Q(`Thermal neutrons in a PWR have energies around…`,[`0.025 eV`,`2 MeV`,`6.67 eV`,`200 MeV`],0,`Room-to-reactor temperature energies.`)]);
TT('t2-2',[x('t2-2-1',Q(`Elastic scattering conserves…`,[`total movement (kinetic) energy`,`the neutron's speed`,`the nucleus`,`nothing`],0,`Like snooker balls.`)),x('t2-2-2',Q(`Cross-sections are measured in…`,[`barns`,`metres`,`becquerels`,`sieverts`],0,`10⁻²⁴ cm².`)),x('t2-2-2',Q(`Zirconium's tiny cross-section makes it good for…`,[`fuel cladding`,`control rods`,`shielding`,`moderation`],0,`Neutrons pass through.`)),x('t2-2-3',Q(`The 1/v rule says…`,[`slower neutrons are absorbed more readily`,`faster neutrons are absorbed more`,`speed doesn't matter`,`all neutrons are absorbed`],0,`σ ∝ 1/v.`)),x('t2-2-3',Q(`What are resonances?`,[`Sharp cross-section peaks at certain energies`,`Vibrations of the core`,`Delayed neutrons`,`Steam bubbles`],0,`Energy-level matches.`)),x('t2-2-1',Q(`How is plutonium-239 made?`,[`U-238 captures a neutron, then decays twice`,`U-235 fissions`,`Boron captures a neutron`,`Xenon decays`],0,`Radiative capture.`))]);

U({id:'t2-3',track:'theory',level:2,title:'The neutron life cycle',icon:'k',color:'#1D9E75',desc:'Follow a generation of neutrons from birth to fission, and meet the six factors that decide k.',lessons:[
LH('t2-3-1','Birth to fission: one generation',10,'Follow 1000 neutrons through every stage of their lives in the core.',[
RM(`<ul><li><b>k</b> is the number of neutrons in one generation divided by the number in the generation before. k = 1 is critical (steady).</li><li>Neutrons can scatter, be captured, cause fission, or <b>leak</b> out of the core.</li><li>U-238 has strong capture <b>resonances</b> at in-between energies.</li></ul>`),
T(`<h3>The plan</h3><p>Let's follow <b>1000 fast neutrons</b>, freshly born from fission, through their lives in a typical PWR core. At each stage, some are lost. The ones that survive to cause fission make the next generation.</p><p>Each stage is described by a <b>factor</b>: a number that multiplies the neutron count. There are six of them.</p>`),
W('stepCalc',{steps:['<b>Start:</b> 1000 fast neutrons from fission.','<b>Fast fission factor ε ≈ 1.03.</b> A few fast neutrons split U-238 straight away, adding neutrons: 1000 × 1.03 = <b>1030</b>.','<b>Fast non-leakage ≈ 0.97.</b> Some fast neutrons escape the core: 1030 × 0.97 = <b>999</b>.','<b>Resonance escape p ≈ 0.75.</b> While slowing down, a quarter are captured in U-238 resonances: 999 × 0.75 = <b>749</b> reach thermal energy.','<b>Thermal non-leakage ≈ 0.99.</b> A few thermal neutrons leak out: 749 × 0.99 = <b>742</b>.','<b>Thermal utilisation f ≈ 0.71.</b> Only 71% are absorbed in the fuel; the rest go into water, cladding, boron, rods: 742 × 0.71 = <b>527</b> absorbed in fuel.','<b>Reproduction factor η ≈ 1.9.</b> Each neutron absorbed in fuel produces 1.9 new neutrons on average: 527 × 1.9 = <b>1001</b>.','<b>Result:</b> 1000 in, about 1000 out. k ≈ <b>1.00</b>: the reactor is critical.']},`Follow 1000 neutrons through one generation.`,`Six factors, multiplied together, give k. Here they come to almost exactly 1.`),
Q(`Which stage loses the most neutrons in this example?`,[`Resonance escape (capture in U-238 while slowing down)`,'Fast leakage (fast neutrons escaping from the core)','Thermal leakage (slow neutrons escaping from the core)','Fast fission (U-238 splitting)'],0,`A quarter are captured in U-238 resonances. Thermal utilisation loses a lot too.`),
T(`<h3>Why so many are "lost"</h3><p>Starting with 1000, only 527 are absorbed in fuel. But each of those makes about 1.9 new neutrons (η), so the population is replaced.</p><p>The control system's job is to keep the product of all six factors exactly at 1.</p>`),
Q(`What does the reproduction factor η mean?`,[`New neutrons made per neutron absorbed in the fuel`,'New neutrons made per fission of U-235','Neutrons that leak out per generation','The share of thermal neutrons that are absorbed in the fuel'],0,`Not every absorption in fuel causes fission (some are captures), so η ≈ 1.9 is less than the 2.4 neutrons per fission.`),
QE(`k = 1 means the reactor is…`,[`critical (steady)`,`subcritical (falling)`,`supercritical (rising)`,`shut down (no fission)`],0,`From Level 1.`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li>Each stage of a neutron's life multiplies the population by a factor.</li><li>Fast fission ε, fast non-leakage, resonance escape p, thermal non-leakage, thermal utilisation f, reproduction η.</li><li>Multiply all six to get <b>k</b>.</li></ul>`)]),
LH('t2-3-2','The six factors',10,'Each factor on its own: what it means and what changes it.',[
RM(`<p>One neutron generation passes through six stages. The factors are: <b>ε</b> (fast fission), <b>fast non-leakage</b>, <b>p</b> (resonance escape), <b>thermal non-leakage</b>, <b>f</b> (thermal utilisation) and <b>η</b> (reproduction). Multiplied together, they give k.</p>`),
T(`<h3>The six-factor formula</h3><div class="eq">k<sub>eff</sub> = ε × L<sub>f</sub> × p × L<sub>t</sub> × f × η</div>${KY([['k<sub>eff</sub>','effective multiplication factor',''],['ε','fast fission factor (≈ 1.03)',''],['L<sub>f</sub>','fast non-leakage probability (≈ 0.97)',''],['p','resonance escape probability (≈ 0.75)',''],['L<sub>t</sub>','thermal non-leakage probability (≈ 0.99)',''],['f','thermal utilisation factor (≈ 0.71)',''],['η','reproduction factor (≈ 1.9)','']],'All are just numbers (no units). Values are typical for a PWR.')}<p>Ignore leakage (an infinitely big core) and you get the four-factor formula for <b>k∞</b> (k-infinity) = ε p f η.</p>`),
W('matchPairs',{pairs:[['ε (fast fission)','Fast neutrons splitting U-238'],['p (resonance escape)','Avoiding capture in U-238 while slowing down'],['f (thermal utilisation)','Share of thermal neutrons absorbed in fuel'],['η (reproduction)','New neutrons per absorption in fuel'],['Non-leakage','Staying inside the core']]},`Match each factor to its meaning.`),
T(`<h3>What changes each factor</h3><ul><li><b>Adding boron or inserting rods</b> → more absorption outside the fuel → <b>f falls</b>.</li><li><b>Hotter fuel</b> → U-238 resonances broaden (Doppler) → <b>p falls</b>.</li><li><b>Hotter, less dense water</b> → slower moderation → more time in the resonances → <b>p falls</b>; and more leakage.</li><li><b>Fuel burn-up</b> → less U-235, more fission products → <b>η and f fall</b>.</li></ul>`),
W('sortIt',{bins:['Mainly lowers f','Mainly lowers p'],items:[['Adding boron to the coolant',0],['Inserting control rods',0],['Fuel getting hotter (Doppler broadening)',1],['Xenon building up in the fuel',0],['Water expanding so neutrons slow down more slowly',1]]},`Which factor does each change mainly reduce?`,`Absorbers (boron, rods, xenon) steal thermal neutrons: f. Temperature effects increase resonance capture: p.`),
QE(`Uranium-238's capture resonances are at energies…`,[`between fast and thermal (a few eV upwards)`,`only at thermal energy, below about 0.1 eV`,`only at fast energies, around 2 MeV and above`,`spread evenly across every neutron energy`],0,`That's why p matters.`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li><b>k = ε × L<sub>f</sub> × p × L<sub>t</sub> × f × η</b>. Without leakage: k∞ = εpfη.</li><li>Absorbers (boron, rods, xenon) mainly reduce <b>f</b>.</li><li>Temperature rises mainly reduce <b>p</b> (and increase leakage): the basis of negative feedback.</li></ul>`)]),
LH('t2-3-3','Leakage and core size',9,'Why a reactor has a minimum size, and why the edges of the core matter.',[
RM(`<p>Two of the six factors are about <b>leakage</b>: neutrons escaping the core before they can cause fission. Fast neutrons travel further, so they leak more.</p>`),
T(`<h3>Surface versus volume</h3><p>Neutrons are <b>made</b> throughout the volume of the core, but they <b>leak</b> through its surface.</p><ul><li>Double the size of a cube: its volume goes up 8 times, but its surface only 4 times.</li><li>So a bigger core leaks a <b>smaller share</b> of its neutrons.</li></ul><p>There's a minimum size, the <b>critical size</b>, below which too many neutrons leak for the chain reaction to keep going.</p>`),
W('fnPlot',{x:[0.5,5],y:[0,7],xl:'size of cube (side length)',yl:'surface ÷ volume',sl:[['s','Side length',0.5,5,0.5,1,function(v){return v}]],curves:function(p){return [[function(a){return 6/a},'--accent',3]]},marks:function(p,P,col){var c=P.c;c.fillStyle=col('--coral');c.beginPath();c.arc(P.X(p.s),P.Y(6/p.s),6,0,7);c.fill()},read:function(p){return 'Side '+p.s+': surface = '+(6*p.s*p.s).toFixed(1)+', volume = '+(p.s*p.s*p.s).toFixed(1)+', surface ÷ volume = <b>'+(6/p.s).toFixed(2)+'</b>. Bigger means proportionally less surface to leak through.'}},`Change the size of a cube-shaped core. Watch the surface-to-volume ratio.`),
T(`<h3>Reflectors and the edge of the core</h3><p>Around the core, a <b>reflector</b> (in a PWR, the water and the steel baffle) bounces some escaping neutrons back in, reducing leakage.</p><p>Leakage also shapes the power: near the edges, neutrons escape, so power is lower there than in the middle. EPR's heavy steel reflector reduces leakage and evens out the power.</p>`),
Q(`Why can't you build a working reactor the size of a football?`,[`Too many neutrons would leak out through its surface`,'It would get far too hot for water to carry the heat away','There would be no room for xenon to build up','Its control rods would be too short to work'],0,`Small things have a lot of surface compared with their volume.`),
Q(`What does a reflector do?`,[`Bounces escaping neutrons back into the core`,'Reflects heat from the fuel back into the core','Absorbs neutrons that leak out','Reflects light so the core can be seen'],0,`Less leakage means less fuel is needed for criticality.`),
QE(`In the six-factor formula, which factor do control rods mainly reduce?`,[`f, thermal utilisation`,`ε, fast fission`,`η, reproduction`,`the non-leakage factors`],0,`Rods absorb thermal neutrons outside the fuel.`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li>Neutrons are made in the volume but leak through the surface.</li><li>Bigger cores leak a smaller share: there's a <b>critical size</b>.</li><li><b>Reflectors</b> send neutrons back in and flatten the power across the core.</li></ul>`)])
]});
F('t2-3-1',[T(`<h3>Quick recap</h3><p>1000 fast → ×ε → ×fast non-leak → ×p → ×thermal non-leak → ×f → ×η → next generation.</p>`),Q(`The last factor, η, is about…`,[`new neutrons made per absorption in fuel`,`leakage`,`slowing down`,`fast fission`],0,`η ≈ 1.9.`),Q(`What is ε (fast fission) typically?`,[`About 1.03`,`About 0.5`,`About 2.4`,`About 0.03`],0,`A small boost.`),Q(`If the product of the six factors is 0.98, the reactor is…`,[`subcritical`,`critical`,`supercritical`,`prompt critical`],0,`k < 1.`)]);
F('t2-3-2',[T(`<h3>Quick recap</h3><p>k = ε L<sub>f</sub> p L<sub>t</sub> f η. Absorbers lower f; heat lowers p.</p>`),Q(`k∞ ignores…`,[`leakage`,`fission`,`moderation`,`absorption`],0,`Infinite core.`),Q(`Adding boron lowers…`,[`f`,`η`,`ε`,`p`],0,`More thermal absorption outside the fuel.`),Q(`Doppler broadening lowers…`,[`p`,`f`,`η`,`ε`],0,`More resonance capture in U-238.`)]);
F('t2-3-3',[T(`<h3>Quick recap</h3><p>Volume makes neutrons, surface leaks them. Bigger = less leakage. Reflectors help.</p>`),Q(`Doubling a cube's side multiplies its volume by…`,[`8`,`4`,`2`,`6`],0,`2³.`),Q(`Which leaks more: fast or thermal neutrons?`,[`Fast`,`Thermal`,`Same`,`Neither leaks`],0,`They travel further before being absorbed.`),Q(`What is the critical size?`,[`The smallest core that can sustain a chain reaction`,`The biggest allowed core`,`The size of a fuel pellet`,`The size of the containment`],0,`Below it, leakage wins.`)]);
TT('t2-3',[x('t2-3-2',Q(`How many factors in the formula for k<sub>eff</sub>?`,[`Six`,`Four`,`Two`,`Ten`],0,`Four for k∞ plus two leakage factors.`)),x('t2-3-1',Q(`Resonance escape probability p is about…`,[`0.75`,`1.9`,`1.03`,`0.01`],0,`About a quarter captured.`)),x('t2-3-2',Q(`Which change lowers f?`,[`Inserting control rods`,`Fuel cooling down`,`A bigger core`,`Less boron`],0,`More absorber outside the fuel.`)),x('t2-3-3',Q(`A reflector reduces…`,[`leakage`,`fission`,`moderation`,`temperature`],0,`Sends neutrons back.`)),x('t2-3-1',Q(`Neutrons absorbed in fuel but not causing fission…`,[`make η smaller than the neutrons per fission`,`increase k`,`don't happen`,`are leakage`],0,`Captures in fuel.`)),x('t2-3-3',Q(`Bigger cores have…`,[`less surface per unit volume`,`more surface per unit volume`,`no leakage at all`,`more leakage`],0,`Surface/volume falls.`))]);

U({id:'t2-4',track:'theory',level:2,title:'Heat, steam and energy',icon:'h',color:'#BA7517',desc:'Internal energy and enthalpy, boiling and latent heat, saturation and steam quality, and the first law.',lessons:[
LH('t2-4-1','Heating water to steam',9,'Why the temperature stops rising while water boils, and where that energy goes.',[
RM(`<ul><li>Water's boiling point depends on <b>pressure</b>: 100 °C at 1 bar, about 345 °C at 155 bar (Level 1, Primary circuit).</li><li>In a steam generator, secondary water boils into steam.</li></ul>`),
T(`<h3>Two kinds of heating</h3><p>Heat a pan of water steadily and watch the thermometer:</p><ol class="steps"><li>The temperature rises steadily up to 100 °C. This is <b>sensible heat</b>: you can sense it as a temperature rise.</li><li>At 100 °C, the water boils. The temperature <b>stops rising</b> until all the water has turned to steam. The energy goes into pulling molecules apart. This is <b>latent heat</b> ("hidden" heat).</li><li>Once it's all steam, the steam's temperature rises again.</li></ol>`),
W('fnPlot',{x:[0,3000],y:[0,200],xl:'energy added (kJ per kg of water, at 1 bar)',yl:'temperature (°C)',sl:[['q','Energy added (kJ/kg)',0,3000,50,200,function(v){return v+' kJ/kg'}]],curves:function(p){return [[function(q){return q<334?20+q/4.18:q<2591?100:100+(q-2591)/2.0},'--accent',3]]},marks:function(p,P,col){var q=p.q,T2=q<334?20+q/4.18:q<2591?100:100+(q-2591)/2.0,c=P.c;c.fillStyle=col('--coral');c.beginPath();c.arc(P.X(q),P.Y(T2),6,0,7);c.fill()},read:function(p){var q=p.q;if(q<334)return 'Heating liquid water: <b>'+(20+q/4.18).toFixed(0)+' °C</b>. Sensible heat.';if(q<2591)return 'Boiling at <b>100 °C</b>: '+Math.round((q-334)/2257*100)+'% turned to steam. The temperature isn\'t changing: this is <b>latent heat</b>.';return 'All steam, now heating up: <b>'+(100+(q-2591)/2.0).toFixed(0)+' °C</b>. Superheated steam.'},goal:function(p){return p.q>=600&&p.q<=2300},goalMsg:'Add energy until the water is boiling, part way through the flat section.'},`Add energy to 1 kg of water starting at 20 °C. Find the flat part where it's boiling.`,`Boiling takes about 2,257 kJ/kg at 1 bar: almost seven times the 334 kJ/kg needed to heat it from 20 °C to 100 °C.`),
Q(`While water boils at a constant pressure, its temperature…`,[`stays the same`,`rises steadily`,`falls`,`goes up and down`],0,`The energy goes into changing liquid to vapour.`),
T(`<h3>Why this matters in a power station</h3><p>Turning water into steam takes a <b>huge</b> amount of energy, and condensing steam back to water gives it all back. That's why:</p><ul><li>steam generators can carry away enormous heat from the primary circuit</li><li>the condenser needs so much sea water: it has to remove all that latent heat</li></ul>`),
Q(`Most of the heat removed in the condenser is…`,[`latent heat, released as steam turns back to water`,'sensible heat, given out as the hot water cools down','heat from the generator, carried away by the water','heat from friction in the turbine, as the blades spin'],0,`Condensing releases the latent heat.`),
QE(`At 155 bar, water boils at about…`,[`345 °C`,`100 °C`,`155 °C`,`1000 °C`],0,`From Level 1.`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li><b>Sensible heat</b> raises temperature. <b>Latent heat</b> changes liquid to vapour at constant temperature.</li><li>At 1 bar, boiling water needs about 2,257 kJ/kg of latent heat.</li><li>Latent heat is why steam carries so much energy and why condensers need so much cooling.</li></ul>`)]),
LH('t2-4-2','Enthalpy and steam tables',10,'The one quantity engineers use to track energy in flowing water and steam.',[
RM(`<ul><li>Sensible heat changes temperature; latent heat changes liquid to vapour.</li><li>Water's boiling (saturation) temperature rises with pressure.</li></ul>`),
T(`<h3>Enthalpy: energy per kilogram</h3><p>Engineers track the energy in water and steam using <b>specific enthalpy</b>, written <b>h</b>, measured in <b>kJ/kg</b>.</p><p>You don't need its exact definition yet. Think of it as: <b>how much energy one kilogram of flowing water or steam carries</b>, measured from a standard starting point.</p><ul><li>Water at 20 °C: h ≈ 84 kJ/kg</li><li>Boiling water at 1 bar (100 °C): h ≈ 419 kJ/kg</li><li>Dry steam at 1 bar (100 °C): h ≈ 2,676 kJ/kg</li></ul>`),
Q(`What does specific enthalpy measure, roughly?`,[`The energy carried by each kilogram of water or steam`,'The temperature of each kilogram of water','The pressure each kilogram of steam pushes on the pipes','The mass of water flowing past each second'],0,`Measured in kJ/kg.`),
T(`<h3>Steam tables</h3><p>The properties of water and steam have been measured very precisely and printed in <b>steam tables</b>. For each pressure they list:</p><table><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Meaning</th></tr><tr><td>T<sub>sat</sub></td><td>boiling (saturation) temperature</td></tr><tr><td>h<sub>f</sub></td><td>enthalpy of saturated water (just about to boil)</td></tr><tr><td>h<sub>g</sub></td><td>enthalpy of saturated steam (just finished boiling)</td></tr><tr><td>h<sub>fg</sub> = h<sub>g</sub> − h<sub>f</sub></td><td>latent heat of boiling</td></tr></table>`),
W('flipCards',{cards:[['1 bar','T<sub>sat</sub> 100 °C · h<sub>f</sub> 419 · h<sub>g</sub> 2,676 kJ/kg'],['70 bar (steam generator)','T<sub>sat</sub> 286 °C · h<sub>f</sub> 1,267 · h<sub>g</sub> 2,773 kJ/kg'],['155 bar (pressuriser)','T<sub>sat</sub> 345 °C · h<sub>f</sub> 1,630 · h<sub>g</sub> 2,596 kJ/kg'],['Pattern','At higher pressure the latent heat h<sub>fg</sub> gets smaller']]},`Flip the cards to see values from the steam tables.`),
T(`<h3>Quality: how much is steam?</h3><p>Partly boiled water is a mix of liquid and vapour. Its <b>quality</b>, x, is the fraction (by mass) that is vapour.</p><div class="eq">h = h<sub>f</sub> + x × h<sub>fg</sub></div>${KY([['h','enthalpy of the mixture','kJ/kg'],['h<sub>f</sub>','enthalpy of saturated water','kJ/kg'],['x','quality: fraction that is steam (0 to 1)',''],['h<sub>fg</sub>','latent heat','kJ/kg']])}<p>Turbine exhaust steam is "wet": typically x ≈ 0.9 (90% steam, 10% droplets).</p>`),
N(`At 1 bar, h<sub>f</sub> = 419 and h<sub>fg</sub> = 2257 kJ/kg. What is h for steam of quality x = 0.5?`,1547.5,0.01,'kJ/kg',`419 + 0.5 × 2257 = 419 + 1128.5 = 1547.5 kJ/kg.`),
QE(`Turning steam back to water in the condenser releases…`,[`latent heat`,`no heat`,`only sensible heat`,`radiation`],0,`From the last lesson.`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li><b>Specific enthalpy h</b> (kJ/kg): the energy each kilogram of water or steam carries.</li><li><b>Steam tables</b> give T<sub>sat</sub>, h<sub>f</sub>, h<sub>g</sub>, h<sub>fg</sub> at each pressure.</li><li><b>Quality x</b> is the steam fraction: h = h<sub>f</sub> + x h<sub>fg</sub>.</li></ul>`)]),
LH('t2-4-3','The first law: energy in = energy out',9,'Energy can\'t be made or destroyed. The simple balance behind every heat calculation in the plant.',[
RM(`<ul><li><b>Enthalpy h</b> is energy carried per kg of flowing water or steam (kJ/kg).</li><li>Mass flow rate <b>ṁ</b> is kilograms flowing per second.</li></ul>`),
T(`<h3>The first law of thermodynamics</h3><p>Energy can't be created or destroyed, only moved or changed in form.</p><p>For something with water flowing steadily through it (a steam generator, a turbine, a condenser):</p><div class="eq">energy in per second = energy out per second</div>`),
T(`<h3>For a component with flow</h3><div class="eq">Q̇ − Ẇ = ṁ (h<sub>out</sub> − h<sub>in</sub>)</div>${KY([['Q̇','heat added per second','kW'],['Ẇ','work taken out per second (e.g. by a turbine)','kW'],['ṁ','mass flow rate','kg/s'],['h<sub>out</sub>, h<sub>in</sub>','enthalpy leaving and entering','kJ/kg']],'kJ/kg × kg/s = kJ/s = kW.')}<ul><li><b>Steam generator</b>: no work, heat added: Q̇ = ṁ (h<sub>out</sub> − h<sub>in</sub>).</li><li><b>Turbine</b>: (almost) no heat, work out: Ẇ = ṁ (h<sub>in</sub> − h<sub>out</sub>).</li></ul>`),
W('stepCalc',{steps:['A turbine takes 2000 kg/s of steam. Enthalpy in: 2,780 kJ/kg. Enthalpy out: 2,300 kJ/kg.','No heat added, so Ẇ = ṁ (h<sub>in</sub> − h<sub>out</sub>)','Ẇ = 2000 × (2780 − 2300)','Ẇ = 2000 × 480 = 960,000 kW','= <b>960 MW</b> of mechanical power to the generator.']},`Work out a turbine's power from the first law.`),
N(`Feedwater enters a steam generator at 1,000 kJ/kg and leaves as steam at 2,770 kJ/kg. Flow is 600 kg/s. How much heat is added, in MW?`,1062,0.01,'MW',`600 × (2770 − 1000) = 600 × 1770 = 1,062,000 kW = 1062 MW.`,`Q̇ = ṁ (h<sub>out</sub> − h<sub>in</sub>), then kW → MW.`),
Q(`In a turbine, why does the steam's enthalpy fall?`,[`Its energy is taken out as work turning the shaft`,'It gains heat from the turbine casing','It is compressed as it goes through','It cools by losing its heat to the metal of the blades'],0,`Energy out as work.`),
QE(`What is the quality of steam that is 80% vapour?`,[`0.8`,`80 kJ/kg`,`0.2`,`8`],0,`x is the vapour fraction.`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li><b>First law</b>: energy in = energy out (for steady flow).</li><li><b>Q̇ − Ẇ = ṁ (h<sub>out</sub> − h<sub>in</sub>)</b>.</li><li>Steam generator: heat in, no work. Turbine: work out, no heat.</li></ul>`)])
]});
F('t2-4-1',[T(`<h3>Quick recap</h3><p>Sensible heat changes temperature; latent heat changes phase at constant temperature.</p>`),Q(`Latent heat is used to…`,[`turn liquid into vapour`,`raise the temperature`,`lower the pressure`,`make neutrons`],0,`Phase change.`),Q(`About how much latent heat does water need at 1 bar?`,[`2,257 kJ/kg`,`4.18 kJ/kg`,`419 kJ/kg`,`100 kJ/kg`],0,`Huge.`),Q(`Steam heated above its boiling point is…`,[`superheated`,`wet`,`saturated water`,`subcooled`],0,`All vapour, hotter than T<sub>sat</sub>.`)]);
F('t2-4-2',[T(`<h3>Quick recap</h3><p>h in kJ/kg. Steam tables: h<sub>f</sub>, h<sub>g</sub>, h<sub>fg</sub>. h = h<sub>f</sub> + x h<sub>fg</sub>.</p>`),N(`h<sub>f</sub> = 1267, h<sub>fg</sub> = 1506 kJ/kg, x = 1. What is h?`,2773,0.01,'kJ/kg',`1267 + 1506.`),Q(`x = 0 means…`,[`saturated water (no steam yet)`,`dry steam`,`superheated steam`,`ice`],0,`No vapour.`),Q(`As pressure rises, h<sub>fg</sub>…`,[`gets smaller`,`gets bigger`,`stays the same`,`becomes negative`],0,`From the tables.`)]);
F('t2-4-3',[T(`<h3>Quick recap</h3><p>Q̇ − Ẇ = ṁ(h<sub>out</sub> − h<sub>in</sub>).</p>`),N(`Turbine: ṁ = 1000 kg/s, h<sub>in</sub> = 2800, h<sub>out</sub> = 2300 kJ/kg. Power in MW?`,500,0.01,'MW',`1000 × 500 kW.`),Q(`In a condenser, Q̇ is…`,[`negative: heat is removed`,`positive`,`zero`,`work`],0,`Enthalpy falls.`),Q(`The first law says energy…`,[`can't be created or destroyed`,`always increases`,`is always lost`,`is the same as power`],0,`Conservation of energy.`)]);
TT('t2-4',[x('t2-4-1',Q(`While water boils, the energy added goes into…`,[`changing liquid to vapour`,`raising temperature`,`raising pressure`,`nothing`],0,`Latent heat.`)),x('t2-4-2',Q(`h is measured in…`,[`kJ/kg`,`°C`,`bar`,`kg/s`],0,`Energy per kg.`)),x('t2-4-2',N(`h<sub>f</sub> = 419, h<sub>fg</sub> = 2257, x = 0.2. h?`,870.4,0.01,'kJ/kg',`419 + 451.4.`)),x('t2-4-3',Q(`For a steam generator, Ẇ is…`,[`zero`,`large`,`negative`,`the flow`],0,`No shaft work.`)),x('t2-4-3',N(`ṁ = 500 kg/s, h rises by 2000 kJ/kg. Heat added in MW?`,1000,0.01,'MW',`500 × 2000 = 1,000,000 kW.`)),x('t2-4-1',Q(`Why does the condenser need so much cooling water?`,[`It must remove all the latent heat`,`The steam is radioactive`,`To keep the sea warm`,`It doesn't`],0,`Condensing releases latent heat.`))]);

U({id:'t2-5',track:'theory',level:2,title:'Materials and water chemistry',icon:'Fe',color:'#D4537E',desc:'What radiation does to metals, how corrosion is controlled, and why PWR water chemistry is so carefully managed.',lessons:[
LH('t2-5-1','What radiation does to metals',9,'Fast neutrons knock atoms out of place. Here\'s what that does to steel over 60 years.',[
RM(`<ul><li>The reactor pressure vessel is about 25 cm of steel (Level 1, Fuel and the core).</li><li>Fast neutrons carry a lot of movement energy.</li><li>In elastic scattering, a neutron hands energy to the nucleus it hits.</li></ul>`),
T(`<h3>Knocking atoms out of place</h3><p>In a metal, atoms sit in a neat repeating pattern called a <b>crystal lattice</b>. When a fast neutron hits one, it can knock it out of its place, and that atom knocks others, like a break in snooker.</p><p>Each metal atom in a reactor vessel is knocked out of place many times over its life. This is measured in <b>displacements per atom (dpa)</b>.</p>`),
T(`<h3>What the damage does</h3><table><tr><th>Effect</th><th>What happens</th><th>Where it matters</th></tr><tr><td><b>Embrittlement</b></td><td>Steel becomes harder but more brittle: it can crack instead of bending</td><td>Reactor pressure vessel</td></tr><tr><td><b>Swelling</b></td><td>Tiny voids form and the metal grows</td><td>Core internals</td></tr><tr><td><b>Irradiation creep</b></td><td>Metal slowly deforms under load</td><td>Fuel cladding, internals</td></tr><tr><td><b>Growth</b></td><td>Zirconium tubes get longer</td><td>Fuel rods and assemblies</td></tr></table>`),
Q(`What does "embrittlement" mean?`,[`The steel becomes more likely to crack than to bend`,'The steel gets softer, so it bends more easily under load','The steel melts at a lower temperature','The steel becomes radioactive over time'],0,`Brittle materials snap rather than stretch.`),
T(`<h3>Watching the vessel age</h3><p>Steel has a <b>transition temperature</b>: above it, steel is tough (it bends); below it, steel is brittle (it cracks). Radiation <b>raises</b> this temperature over the years.</p><p>To track it, small <b>surveillance specimens</b> of the same steel are placed inside the vessel, closer to the core so they age faster. They're removed and tested every few years, predicting the vessel's future.</p><div class="callout">This is why operators must never cool the vessel too fast while it's pressurised: <b>pressurised thermal shock</b> could crack embrittled steel.</div>`),
Q(`Why are surveillance specimens placed closer to the core than the vessel wall?`,[`They age faster, so they show in advance how the vessel will behave`,'To shield the vessel wall from some of the neutrons','To measure the temperature right next to the core','To hold the fuel assemblies in place and stop them moving during running'],0,`A preview of the vessel's future.`),
QE(`Which neutrons travel furthest and do most damage to the vessel?`,[`Fast neutrons`,`Thermal neutrons`,`Delayed neutrons`,`None`],0,`They carry the most energy.`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li>Fast neutrons knock atoms out of place: measured in <b>dpa</b>.</li><li>Effects: <b>embrittlement</b>, swelling, creep and growth.</li><li>Radiation raises the steel's <b>transition temperature</b>; surveillance specimens track it.</li><li>Avoid cooling a pressurised vessel too fast (<b>pressurised thermal shock</b>).</li></ul>`)]),
LH('t2-5-2','Corrosion',9,'Hot water slowly eats metal. How corrosion happens, and why it spreads radioactivity.',[
RM(`<ul><li>The primary circuit carries water at about 300 °C and 155 bar.</li><li>Materials near the core capture neutrons and become radioactive (<b>activation</b>).</li></ul>`),
T(`<h3>Corrosion basics</h3><p>Metals in hot water slowly react with it, forming <b>oxides</b> on their surface (rust is iron oxide). A thin, tough oxide layer can protect the metal underneath, but:</p><ul><li>some metal still dissolves into the water</li><li>oxide flakes can break off and travel round the circuit</li></ul>`),
T(`<h3>Why corrosion means radiation</h3><p>Tiny particles of corroded metal circulate with the water. When they pass through the core, they capture neutrons and become radioactive. Then they settle out elsewhere, such as in the steam generators.</p><ul><li>Nickel-58 → <b>cobalt-58</b> (half-life 71 days)</li><li>Cobalt-59 → <b>cobalt-60</b> (half-life 5.3 years, strong gamma)</li></ul><p>These deposits are the main source of radiation dose to workers during outages. Deposits on the fuel are called <b>crud</b>.</p>`),
W('stepCalc',{steps:['A steam generator tube surface corrodes slightly. Nickel atoms enter the water.','The water carries them to the core.','Some settle on the fuel as <b>crud</b>, where they capture neutrons: nickel-58 becomes cobalt-58.','Crud flakes off and travels round the circuit again.','It settles in the steam generators and pipework, where workers will later carry out maintenance.','Result: <b>radiation fields</b> around the primary circuit, from radioactive corrosion products.']},`Follow corrosion products around the circuit.`),
Q(`Why do plants limit cobalt in their materials?`,[`Cobalt-59 becomes cobalt-60, a long-lived strong gamma emitter`,'Cobalt is too expensive to use in large amounts','Cobalt absorbs too many neutrons, so the reactor runs down','It doesn\'t really matter; it\'s just to save money'],0,`Cobalt-60 dominates outage doses at many plants.`),
T(`<h3>Stress corrosion cracking</h3><p>When a metal is under stress <b>and</b> in a corrosive environment, cracks can grow slowly through it. This <b>stress corrosion cracking</b> has affected steam generator tubes and nozzles in many plants. It's controlled by careful choice of materials (like Alloy 690 tubing) and water chemistry.</p>`),
QE(`What is crud?`,[`Deposits of corrosion products on the fuel`,`A kind of fuel pellet with extra gadolinium`,`Used fuel stored in the cooling pond`,`Boric acid that has crystallised in the pipes`],0,`From earlier in this lesson.`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li>Hot water slowly corrodes metals; oxide particles circulate.</li><li>In the core, they become radioactive (cobalt-58, cobalt-60): the main source of outage dose.</li><li><b>Crud</b> is corrosion deposit on fuel. <b>Stress corrosion cracking</b> needs stress + corrosive water.</li></ul>`)]),
LH('t2-5-3','PWR water chemistry',10,'Boron, lithium, hydrogen: the careful recipe that keeps the primary circuit healthy.',[
RM(`<ul><li><b>Boric acid</b> is dissolved in primary water to control reactivity (Level 1).</li><li>Corrosion products become radioactive in the core and cause worker dose.</li></ul>`),
T(`<h3>The problem with boric acid</h3><p>Boric acid makes the water slightly <b>acidic</b>. Acidic water corrodes steel and nickel alloys faster, making more radioactive crud.</p><p>The fix: add a small amount of an alkali, <b>lithium hydroxide</b>, to balance it. The goal is a slightly alkaline water, around <b>pH 7.2</b> at operating temperature.</p>`),
T(`<h3>Lithium follows boron</h3><p>Over a fuel cycle, boron is gradually reduced (from about 1,000+ ppm to near zero). As boron falls, less lithium is needed. Chemists adjust lithium to stay on a "coordinated" boron–lithium curve.</p><p>(Lithium-7 is used, because lithium-6 would absorb neutrons and make tritium.)</p>`),
T(`<h3>Keeping oxygen out</h3><p>Radiation splits water molecules, making oxygen and hydrogen peroxide, which are very corrosive. So a little <b>dissolved hydrogen</b> is added to the primary water. It recombines with the oxygen as fast as it forms.</p><p>Chemists also add <b>zinc</b> at some plants: it builds into the oxide layer and reduces how much cobalt gets in, lowering radiation fields.</p>`),
W('matchPairs',{pairs:[['Boric acid','Controls reactivity, but makes water acidic'],['Lithium hydroxide','Raises the pH to reduce corrosion'],['Dissolved hydrogen','Removes oxygen made by radiation'],['Zinc','Reduces cobalt in the oxide layer'],['Lithium-7','Used instead of lithium-6, which makes tritium']]},`Match each chemical to its job in the primary water.`),
Q(`Why is lithium hydroxide added to the primary water?`,[`To counteract the acidity of boric acid and reduce corrosion`,'To absorb neutrons and help the boron control power','To cool the water, so it stays well below boiling','To make tritium, which is sold as a valuable by-product'],0,`It keeps the pH slightly alkaline.`),
QE(`As fuel burns up over a cycle, the boron concentration…`,[`is gradually reduced`,`is increased`,`stays the same`,`is removed on day one`],0,`From Level 1. Lithium is reduced to match.`),
T(`<h3>Key points</h3><ul><li>Boric acid makes water acidic; <b>lithium hydroxide</b> brings pH up to about 7.2.</li><li>Lithium is reduced as boron falls: a <b>coordinated</b> chemistry.</li><li><b>Hydrogen</b> removes radiation-made oxygen; <b>zinc</b> can reduce radiation fields.</li></ul>`)])
]});
F('t2-5-1',[T(`<h3>Quick recap</h3><p>Fast neutrons → displaced atoms (dpa) → embrittlement, swelling, creep, growth.</p>`),Q(`Radiation makes the steel's transition temperature…`,[`rise`,`fall`,`stay the same`,`disappear`],0,`It becomes brittle at higher temperatures.`),Q(`What does dpa stand for?`,[`Displacements per atom`,`Dose per area`,`Decay per atom`,`Density per area`],0,`How many times each atom has been knocked out of place.`),Q(`Pressurised thermal shock is a risk when…`,[`a pressurised vessel is cooled too quickly`,`the reactor trips`,`the turbine trips`,`boron is added`],0,`Thermal stress on embrittled steel.`)]);
F('t2-5-2',[T(`<h3>Quick recap</h3><p>Corrosion particles → activated in core (Co-58, Co-60) → deposit elsewhere → dose.</p>`),Q(`Cobalt-60's half-life is about…`,[`5.3 years`,`71 days`,`8 days`,`30 years`],0,`Long-lived.`),Q(`Stress corrosion cracking needs…`,[`stress and a corrosive environment`,`only heat`,`only radiation`,`only pressure`],0,`Both together.`),Q(`Most outage worker dose comes from…`,[`activated corrosion products`,`the fuel directly`,`the turbine`,`the sea`],0,`Deposits in the primary circuit.`)]);
F('t2-5-3',[T(`<h3>Quick recap</h3><p>Boron (acidic) + lithium (alkali) → pH ≈ 7.2. Hydrogen scavenges oxygen. Zinc reduces cobalt.</p>`),Q(`Why is lithium-7 used, not lithium-6?`,[`Lithium-6 absorbs neutrons and makes tritium`,`Lithium-7 is cheaper`,`Lithium-6 is acidic`,`No reason`],0,`Avoiding tritium.`),Q(`Dissolved hydrogen is added to…`,[`remove oxygen made by radiation`,`control reactivity`,`raise pressure`,`cool the core`],0,`Prevents corrosive conditions.`),Q(`As boron is reduced, lithium is…`,[`reduced too`,`increased`,`left the same`,`removed completely on day one`],0,`Coordinated chemistry.`)]);
TT('t2-5',[x('t2-5-1',Q(`Embrittlement makes the vessel steel…`,[`more likely to crack`,`softer`,`thicker`,`non-metallic`],0,`Brittle.`)),x('t2-5-1',Q(`Surveillance specimens are used to…`,[`predict how the vessel steel will age`,`measure flow`,`shield workers`,`hold fuel`],0,`They age faster.`)),x('t2-5-2',Q(`Cobalt-60 comes from…`,[`cobalt-59 capturing a neutron`,`fission`,`boron`,`uranium decay`],0,`Activation.`)),x('t2-5-2',Q(`Crud is…`,[`corrosion deposits on fuel`,`spent fuel`,`a coolant`,`a type of steel`],0,`Chalk River Unidentified Deposits, originally.`)),x('t2-5-3',Q(`Target primary pH at temperature is about…`,[`7.2`,`3`,`12`,`1`],0,`Slightly alkaline.`)),x('t2-5-3',Q(`Why add hydrogen to primary water?`,[`To scavenge oxygen from radiolysis`,`To add reactivity`,`To make steam`,`To increase pH`],0,`Prevents corrosion.`))]);


